*   Topic: Calendar overload and identifying an accessibility blind spot.
    *   Format: Substack Note.
    *   Length: 60-100 words.
    *   Style: Plain text, one or two short paragraphs.
    *   Constraints: No hashtags, no title.

    *   Calendar overload (back-to-back meetings) isn't just a productivity issue.
    *   It affects people with neurodivergent traits (ADHD, autism) or those with disabilities who need transition time, decompressing, or physical breaks.
    *   "Back-to-back" is a barrier to accessibility.

    We often talk about calendar overload regarding productivity, but we rarely discuss it as an accessibility blind spot. When we schedule back-to-back meetings without buffers, we are inadvertently excluding neurodivergent colleagues or those with physical disabilities who need transition time. A five-minute gap isn't just a breathing break for some; it's a necessity for others to decompress or manage energy levels. If we want to build truly inclusive workplaces, we have to start respecting the space between the blocks.

    *   *Word count check:* 88 words.
    *   *Format check:* One paragraph, no hashtags, no title.
    *   *Content check:* Addresses calendar overload as an accessibility blind spot.

